we were tired  we were scared felt at a loss it didn't  matter what you  what you were doing you were losing  people  you you had people crying saying just  let me die i don't want to be here  bridget could only watch in horror as  the virus spread  across the country a lifetime in care  work  but nothing could prepare her for the  devastation  that was to come we knew there was a  virus out there  everyone was ten on tender hooks because  we thought if it got in the home it  spread like wildfire which it did  didn't matter what you done or  how you tried to avoid it i mean we were  all  going by government guidelines wearing  ppe  and it was keeping people isolated  but obviously it got in  the virus infected the first residence  at the birchwood care home  in march it was just bedlam  um obviously people weren't coming in so  you sat  holding someone's hand um  and you go home in the evening and you  come back the next morning you've lost  three  the cruel solitary deaths one after  another  were taking their toll on bridget and  her colleagues  well we became like their family i mean  um some of us spent more to oh  sorry okay take your time  okay yeah  some of us spent more time at work than  we did at home  and we looked after those people as if  they  were our own one of our and it was  like losing one of your own i held their  round  i held their hand i'm not letting anyone  i careful die by themselves  the care home lost a quarter of its  residents  13 vulnerable lives taken by the  pandemic  caring for three three gentlemen  who were or seriously  seriously very very poorly  um  to listen to someone choking  you know clearing their throats and  being hot delirious  crying  and then coming back the next morning  and all three  gone  that sense of grief being felt by  bridget will be shared by  the colleagues and staff who work with  her here at  birchwood but the sense of loss  will be felt in care homes right  across the country  bridget is brave to share h